=== SCHEMA ===
migrations/000171_charging_si.up.sql defines, in order:

  -- defensive cleanup (self-healing if a stale legacy snapshot is reapplied)
  DROP MATERIALIZED VIEW IF EXISTS cagg_charging_summary CASCADE;
  DROP TABLE IF EXISTS charge_telemetry_readings CASCADE;
  DROP TABLE IF EXISTS charging_telemetry CASCADE;
  DROP TABLE IF EXISTS charging_sessions CASCADE;

  CREATE TABLE charging_telemetry ( ... PRIMARY KEY (vehicle_id, ts) );
    19 columns total, all SI-canonical:
      vehicle_id BIGINT, ts TIMESTAMPTZ, session_id BIGINT (nullable,
        no FK constraint — see WHY below)
      ac_charging_power_w, dc_charging_power_w           — Watts
      ac_charging_energy_in_wh, dc_charging_energy_in_wh — Watt-hours
      charger_voltage_v                                  — Volts
      charger_actual_current_a, charger_pilot_current_a  — Amperes
      charger_phases INTEGER                             — count
      battery_heater_on BOOLEAN, battery_heater_power_w  — Bool + Watts
      charge_limit_soc_pct                               — percent (0-100)
      charge_request, fast_charger_type,
      charging_cable_type, charge_port_latch             — TEXT (open enum)
      charge_port_door_open BOOLEAN

    SELECT create_hypertable('charging_telemetry', 'ts',
        chunk_time_interval => INTERVAL '7 days',
        if_not_exists       => TRUE);
    CREATE INDEX charging_telemetry_vehicle_ts ON
        charging_telemetry (vehicle_id, ts DESC);
    CREATE INDEX charging_telemetry_session_ts ON
        charging_telemetry (session_id, ts) WHERE session_id IS NOT NULL;

  CREATE TABLE charging_sessions ( id BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY, ... );
    19 columns total, all SI-canonical:
      id BIGSERIAL PK, vehicle_id BIGINT NOT NULL
      started_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL, ended_at TIMESTAMPTZ
      start_soc_pct, end_soc_pct, delta_soc_pct           — percent (0-100)
      start_odometer_m, end_odometer_m                    — meters
      start_lat, start_lng                                — decimal degrees
      start_place TEXT
      total_energy_added_wh                               — Watt-hours
      peak_power_w, avg_power_w                           — Watts
      cost_decimal NUMERIC(12,4), cost_currency CHAR(3)   — preserved
      charger_type TEXT, cable_type TEXT                  — open enums
    CREATE INDEX charging_sessions_vehicle_started ON
        charging_sessions (vehicle_id, started_at DESC);
    (regular table — NOT a hypertable.)

WHY DROP MATERIALIZED VIEW + DROP TABLE at the top:
  Two-wave legacy state at the time this migration runs:
    * 000142_baseline_typed created `charging_telemetry` (hypertable
      with kWh / mph / miles columns) and `charging_sessions` (regular
      table with kWh / miles columns). It also created the cagg
      `cagg_charging_summary` over `charging_telemetry`.
    * 000146_drop_snapshot_tables dropped charging_telemetry CASCADE
      (which also took out cagg_charging_summary and
      charge_telemetry_readings via FK + dependent-view CASCADE), but
      did NOT drop charging_sessions — the legacy regular table still
      lingers (with the 000150 column additions
      max_charger_voltage / charger_phases / cable_type).
  This migration's defensive DROP block therefore:
    - re-drops cagg_charging_summary (idempotent NOTICE if absent),
    - re-drops charge_telemetry_readings (idempotent NOTICE if absent),
    - drops the residual legacy charging_sessions wholesale,
    - re-drops charging_telemetry (already gone after 000146).
  CASCADE is safe — a `grep REFERENCES (charging_sessions|charging_telemetry)`
  over migrations/ found exactly two FK references (charging_telemetry
  in 000142 and charge_telemetry_readings in 000021), both already
  superseded; no other live table depends on either. The pre-phase-42
  pl/pgsql analytics functions (fn_battery_charge_cycles,
  fn_charging_*_distribution, fn_true_cost_*, fn_weekly_activity) do
  reference these tables by name but pl/pgsql functions have no DB-
  level dependency tracking — they will fail at runtime against the
  new schema, which is the documented forward-only cost (ADR-004).
  A follow-up phase-42 prompt rewrites or removes those functions.

WHY session_id has no FK constraint:
  The original 000142 schema deferred the FK from charging_telemetry to
  charging_sessions because charging_sessions did not exist when
  charging_telemetry was created (forward dependency). In phase-42,
  the writer attributes a tick to a session by id, but:
    1. The hot per-tick write path must not block on a referential
       check against a row that may not yet exist.
    2. A tick can legitimately precede the corresponding charging_sessions
       row by a few writes (the session row is inserted by the session-
       completion writer, not the per-tick writer).
  A plain BIGINT preserves the join semantics without those costs. The
  session_id index (partial, WHERE session_id IS NOT NULL) covers the
  common per-session telemetry-fetch query.

WHY column-name suffix carries the unit (ADR-004 #4):
  Every unit-bearing numeric column lives in canonical SI and the unit
  suffix is part of the column name. This makes the SI contract self-
  documenting and audit-grep-able. The forbidden-suffix scan in the
  gate enforces the "no wire-format units in column names" rule.

WHY no FK from charge_port_latch / charge_request / fast_charger_type
WHY enum columns are TEXT not closed CHECK constraints:
  Tesla's Fleet Telemetry frequently introduces new enum values (e.g.
  ChargingState.NoPower, ChargeRequest.* additions) without prior
  notice. ADR-004 #6 mandates that vendor enum tokens persist as TEXT
  with no DB-level CHECK constraint so an unexpected value writes
  rather than rejects; the protomodel + parsing layer enforces the
  closed-enum contract upstream.

WHY power and energy columns are not converted to anything else:
  - Watts (W) is the SI base unit for power (J/s). ChargerPower in kW
    is converted to W by units.ToSI in normalize.toSI.
  - Watt-hours (Wh) is the codebase canonical SI form for energy
    (1 Wh = 3600 J; ADR-004 #4 names Wh as the canonical energy unit
    so charge-energy values stay readable as integers / small decimals
    without scientific notation).
  - Volts (V) and Amperes (A) are SI base units; no conversion.

WHY cost_decimal stays as NUMERIC(12,4):
  Money requires decimal precision; the prompt explicitly preserves
  cost_decimal + cost_currency for cost-tracking analytics. NUMERIC(12,4)
  matches the legacy charging_sessions.cost type from 000142.

WHY no compression / retention policies in this migration:
  Phase-42 prompts 0030 (positions) and 0031 (snapshots) deliberately
  omitted compression and retention policies, deferring those operational
  decisions to a later prompt. This migration follows the same convention
  so the policy-tuning prompt can apply them uniformly across all hot
  tables.

